  "headline": "The Hardware Outran the State",
  "regional_lead": "The Nordic-Baltic states have been rearmament's most committed practitioners for three years. This week showed that their real gaps lie not in defence budgets but in the detection systems, civil-warning infrastructure, and political durability that military spending assumes but cannot itself provide. Drones reached or threatened three NATO members in a single week; one government fell over a drone incident; and a survey found 80% of Finns no longer trust Washington to defend European allies — a finding hard to square with a 40-year commitment to the American F-35.\n\nThe drone incidents cut the deepest. Finland's Interior Ministry issued an emergency warning across the Helsinki region at 3:49am after intelligence suggested Ukrainian drones might stray into Finnish airspace; the airport closed and the defence minister authorised the use of force. The all-clear came by morning, but the civil-protection failure was plain — citizens had no text-message alert, no guidance on whether to send children to school, and conflicting statements from officials about whether any drone had crossed the border. Lithuania's problem runs deeper: it cannot verify a drone's origin before it lands on its soil. In the same week its government declared an emergency over a sustained Belarusian balloon campaign that shut Vilnius airport twice, a suspected Ukrainian drone crashed on Lithuanian territory — a navigational failure, apparently, but the distinction matters little when the state cannot tell the difference in real time. The region has invested heavily in what it does after detection; it has invested less in detection itself.\n\nLatvia is the starkest case. Its government fell in five days, and the sequence began with a single drone incident in Rēzekne — specifically, a one-hour detection delay that cost a defence minister his post. The Progressives pulled out of the coalition, and Evika Siliņa resigned as prime minister on the morning investigators raided and briefly detained a sitting cabinet minister for the first time in the country's history. A new prime minister candidate was nominated within 48 hours — the constitutional machinery worked — but the defence ministry has cycled through three leadership arrangements in under two weeks, the government has announced no counter-drone procurement despite a second airspace alert in eastern Latvia on May 15, and analysts openly doubt the nominee can assemble a viable majority. Latvia simultaneously lost its head of government and the head of its civil service. Into that vacuum, Volodymyr Zelensky named Latvia as a potential Russian target through Belarus — the first time a foreign head of state had done so, whatever its precise intelligence basis.\n\nWashington made the picture sharper still. Lithuania's foreign minister confirmed that the US had lobbied Vilnius to reopen Klaipėda port to Belarusian potash — trading sanctions softening for relationship maintenance. Gitanas Nausėda, the president, denied formal pressure while acknowledging the diplomatic exchange had taken place, a careful formulation that preserved the relationship without yielding the point. Lithuania has long treated its alliance with Washington as principled; the US is treating it as transactional. Finland's polling makes the same point differently. Alexander Stubb, the president, called for talks with Russia and urged Europeans to calm down over American troop withdrawals from Germany; his defence minister countered that Russia's continued full mobilisation is not the behaviour of a country seeking peace. The disagreement between them matters less than what drives it: the security guarantee looks less certain, and Finnish public opinion has reached that conclusion faster than Finnish policy has.\n\nEstonia shows what the region is building in response. Its expanded Chunmoo rocket order means Russian territory now lies within reach of Estonian ground-based strike capability for the first time. Norway has formalised a doctrine that its most sensitive defence exports go only to allies, absorbing Malaysian legal pressure to enforce it. Sweden launched its first military surveillance satellite and deepened ties with India as a hedge against American unpredictability. But Estonia's procurement plan was priced before equipment costs rose more than 50% across the alliance, and the Rail Baltica funding gap — covering the military mobility corridor that this week's Kevadtorm exercise was designed to test — remains unresolved for the fourth consecutive week. The region is acquiring strike capabilities that were unthinkable three years ago. What it has not yet built is the detection infrastructure, the civil-protection systems, and the political resilience to absorb the pressure those capabilities are meant to answer. Until it does, the hardware leads and the rest scrambles to catch up.",
  "gap_paragraphs": [
    "Notably absent this week: Coordinated Nordic-Baltic response to US troop rotation pause — institutional burden-sharing adjustment. The US troop rotation pause creates a collective security gap for the eastern flank that the regional framework predicts should activate coordinated response through NORDEFCO, NB8, or B9 channels. The fragmented bilateral responses — Lithuania and Latvia offering individually, others silent — suggests either the coordination mechanism was not activated this week or the response is being processed through NATO channels not captured in country-level reporting. The absence is analytically significant: the most exposed countries (LT, LV) responded individually while the B9 summit was occurring simultaneously. This could indicate a collection gap in how bilateral defense coordination is being tracked, or it could indicate that the B9 framework is not yet the primary vehicle for this type of response.",
    "Notably absent this week: Rail Baltica as an active regional connectivity and military mobility priority during a week when Kevadtorm validated heavy equipment integration at scale. The exercise-validated need for heavy equipment transit from continental Europe (Kevadtorm, Estonia) is now structurally in tension with the absence of Rail Baltica progress across all three Baltic countries simultaneously. The gap between the military mobility validation (Kevadtorm) and the funding impasse (Rail Baltica) is widening, and this week's reporting suggests the project has been crowded out of national agendas by acute crises in Latvia and Lithuania rather than resolved. A regional synthesis that fails to surface this tension — capability validation without enabling infrastructure — would miss one of the most consequential structural dynamics in the Baltic security architecture.",
    "Notably absent this week: Sweden and Finland defense-industrial cooperation following Aurora 26's drone gap finding and Finland's Häkkänen promotion of Finnish firms at EU level. Sweden has a documented drone capability gap and Finland has a drone-capability-adjacent firm (Iceye, which produces surveillance satellites used in drone targeting) being actively promoted by the Finnish Defence Minister. The absence of any bilateral contact between the two countries on this specific gap-capability match is notable — either the connection is being pursued through channels below the threshold of country-desk visibility, or the EU Defence Ministers meeting served as the coordination venue and the outcome is not yet reported, or the gap and the capability promotion are genuinely independent tracks that haven't been matched. Worth monitoring in subsequent weeks as a potential procurement interaction effect."
  ],
  "card_summary": "The Nordic-Baltic states are arming faster than they can warn, shelter, or trust their closest ally."
